dialogue cohesion_engineer_sage
title: Why the lantern burns
trigger: Cohesion engineer sage level=2
once
speaker commander $commander
speaker engineer engineer
speaker sage sage

node open commander
  The Engineer is on the wagon's step with a screwdriver, staring up at the lantern on its hook. The Sage sits on the step below, holding the Engineer's ankle in a way that is not quite friendly.
  -> oil

node oil engineer
  There's no oil in it. I checked the well twice. No wick either, not a proper one. It's been burning since the spire and it doesn't flicker. Sage, let go of my foot.
  -> road

node road sage
  On the Lantern Road there's one rule older than the rest. You don't open a lantern to ask it why it burns. It might answer. Then you'd have to live with what it said.
  -> answer

node answer engineer
  I'd love it to answer. That's the whole point of opening things. Commander, the Sage is sitting on my question.
  * Open it, Engineer. The Sage holds the light steady. -> open_it
    do: AddCohesion engineer sage 10
    do: AddTrust sage -3
    do: SetFlag engineer_sage:lantern_opened
  * Measure it from outside. Nobody opens a road lantern. -> outside
    do: AddCohesion engineer sage 6
    do: SetFlag engineer_sage:lantern_measured
  * Leave the lantern be. Both of you, to bed. -> bed
    do: AddCohesion engineer sage -4
    do: AddTrust engineer -2
    do: SetFlag engineer_sage:lantern_left
  * The Sage hung a knot on that lantern. It's the Sage's to say. -> knot
    if: FlagSet sage:end_wagon
    do: AddCohesion engineer sage 8
    do: AddTrust sage 2
    do: SetFlag engineer_sage:lantern_knot

node open_it sage
  Steady. Fine. If it says anything, Engineer, you're the one writing it down, and I'm not hearing it. Go on. Gently.
  -> reads

node outside engineer
  From outside. The gauge'll still read through glass if I hold it close. Sage, you can keep the ankle. I suppose it helps.
  -> reads

node bed sage
  Bed. I'll sleep under it, so nobody climbs up in the night. Nobody, Engineer. One look, from the ground, and then bed.
  -> reads

node knot sage
  Mine to say. Then the gauge can look. The screwdriver can't. And if the needle so much as wobbles near my knot, we stop.
  -> reads

node reads engineer
  It reads warm. Old warm. Same as the orb near the Commander. Same as Tap Seven before it closed. Whatever the Choir runs on, this is a cousin of it, burning on our wagon. What do we do with that?
  * If the Choir's work runs on the same warmth, this is a road up the stair. Write it for Wick. -> wick
    if: FlagSet sage:wick_promised
    do: AddCohesion engineer sage 10
    do: AddTrust sage 2
    do: SetFlag engineer_sage:warm_wick
  * Together, like the debt. The Engineer writes the numbers, the Sage writes what it mends. -> together
    if: FlagSet engineer:debt_shared
    do: AddCohesion engineer sage 8
    do: SetFlag engineer_sage:warm_together
  * Two records, same night. A page in the notebook and a knot on the cord. -> records
    do: AddCohesion engineer sage 8
    do: SetFlag engineer_sage:warm_records
  * Some things work, and that's enough. Close the notebook. -> enough
    do: AddCohesion engineer sage 2
    do: AddTrust engineer -2
    do: SetFlag engineer_sage:warm_enough

node wick sage
  For Wick. Wick would have had the lid off before you'd finished the sentence, Engineer. You'd have got on. I'd have despaired of you both.
  -> close

node together engineer
  Numbers on the left page, mending on the right. Sage, your handwriting had better be better than your knots.
  -> close

node records sage
  A knot for a lantern. I've never tied one for a thing that wasn't a person. Loose, I think. It's still burning.
  -> close

node enough engineer
  Enough. It works. That's the sentence I hate most in the world, Commander. I'll say it. I won't write it.
  -> close

node close commander
  The lantern goes on burning. The Engineer's pencil and the Sage's cord lie side by side on the step, and above them the light doesn't flicker once.
